  "name": {
    "en": "Senior kyu grades (USJF)"
  },
  "organisation": "United States Judo Federation",
  "country": "US",
  "order": 9,
  "description": {
    "en": "The United States Judo Federation's senior kyu grades for judoka aged 17 and over: six grades from Rokkyu (white belt) to Ikkyu (brown belt). From Sankyu, time in grade depends on whether the candidate is a competitor and on promotion points."
  },
  "source": {
    "title": "Rank Requirement Recommendations Handbook, Section VI",
    "date": "1 May 2021",
    "note": {
      "en": "Transcribed from the senior promotional guidelines in Section VI of the federation's Rank Requirement Recommendations Handbook (5/1/21), which is the authority. Item text is as printed, including the source's own slips (\"Morote Seionage\", \"Name-juji-jime\" for Nami-juji-jime, \"ability of from previous rank\"). Where a line prints a list, each entry is one item and the separating commas are dropped; field labels such as \"Nage Waza:\" become section titles. Belt colours come from the Senior (17 and over) column of the handbook's ranking system table. The handbook disagrees with itself at Nikyu: the text gives Competitor 10 pts 3 months and Batsu-gun 20 pts 0 months, while the handbook's own minimum time in grade grid gives 15 pts 0 months and no Batsu-gun figure. The general rules on promotion points, kata competition points, Batsu-gun promotion and time in grade reductions for contribution are scheme-wide and are not transcribed here. Hadaka-juji-jime, Okuri-eri-juji-jime (Yonkyu) and the Sankaku-gatame printed under Osaekomi Waza at Nikyu are left unresolved: the first two are not standard Kodokan names, and the third is listed as a hold, while the Sankaku-gatame documented here is the armlock. The 2008 study guides are linked as the federation's older companions, and each says it sets no rank standard of its own. They differ from the 2021 handbook: the Yonkyu guide calls the belt Green or Blue and leaves time in grade to the instructor; the Sankyu guide gives 9 months as a non-competitor (the handbook gives 5 months) and competitor times of 6, 3 and 0 months; the Nikyu guide gives one year as a non-competitor (the handbook gives 9 months); the Ikkyu guide gives 2 years, 1 year, 9 months, 6 months and 3 months (the handbook gives 1 year, 9, 6, 3 and 0 months). Their technique checklists also follow an older allocation, for example De-ashi-barai, Tai-otoshi, O-goshi and Seoi-nage at Yonkyu."
    }
  },
